# Build context for the MCP image, shipped with the Dockerfile so every
# consumer gets the same slim upload:
#
#   fly deploy -c fly.mcp.toml \
#     --ignorefile hydra/packages/inka-mcp/Dockerfile.dockerignore
#
# The filename matters: BuildKit picks up `<dockerfile-path>.dockerignore`
# automatically, so this one name serves both a local `docker build -f
# hydra/packages/inka-mcp/Dockerfile .` and the explicit --ignorefile flyctl
# needs (flyctl builds the context itself and does not apply the convention).
#
# This Dockerfile COPYs ONE file -- the bundle -- so everything else in a
# consumer's repo was being uploaded and discarded. One consumer measured its
# MCP context at three megabytes of files the image never reads.
#
# An ALLOW-LIST (`*` then `!path`), so a new directory stays out until
# something names it. The bundle lands in different places depending on who
# builds: Inka's own is dist/, a consumer's is mcp/bundle/ (the path its
# BUNDLE arg names). Both are allowed; the one a given build does not have
# matches nothing.
*
!dist/inka-mcp-http.mjs
!mcp/bundle
